DIFF(1)                        P32W Unix                         DIFF(1)

NAME
       diff - the differences between two files or directory trees

SYNOPSIS
       diff [-u | -U n | -c | -C n | -e] [-bwiqrN] file1 file2

DESCRIPTION
       diff finds the fewest lines to delete from file1 and add to
       it to make file2 (a shortest edit script, by Myers' O(ND)
       algorithm in linear space) and prints them. A file named -
       is the standard input. If one name is a directory and the
       other a file, the file of the same name in the directory is
       compared.

       The normal output is a list of changes, each a command like
       ed's and the lines it involves:

              3a4,5      after line 3 of file1, add lines 4-5 of file2
              7,8d6      delete lines 7-8 (file2 goes on from line 6)
              10c11      change line 10 into line 11

       followed by the lines of file1 marked "< ", then "---" (for a
       change), then the lines of file2 marked "> ". A last line
       without a newline is followed by

              \ No newline at end of file

       Two directories are compared file by file, in name order:
       names in only one are listed (Only in dir: name), files that
       differ are compared as above after a line "diff file1 file2",
       and subdirectories present in both are mentioned (Common
       subdirectories: ...) or, with -r, compared in turn.

OPTIONS
       -u     unified output: a header (--- file1 date, +++ file2
              date), then each group of changes with 3 lines of
              context in a hunk headed @@ -l,n +l,n @@; lines are
              marked " " (the same), "-" (only in file1) and "+"
              (only in file2)
       -U n   unified output with n lines of context
       -c     context output: a header (*** file1 date, --- file2
              date), then for each group the lines of file1
              (*** l,m ****) and of file2 (--- l,m ----) with 3 lines
              of context, changed lines marked "! ", deleted "- ",
              added "+ "
       -C n   context output with n lines of context
       -e     an ed script that turns file1 into file2: the changes
              from the last to the first, so that the line numbers
              stay right. A line of a single dot is written as two
              and put right by an s command after the text.
       -b     ignore changes in the amount of white space: runs of
              blanks and tabs compare equal to one blank, and white
              space at the ends of lines is ignored
       -w     ignore all white space
       -i     ignore the case of letters
       -q     only say whether the files differ (Files a and b
              differ), not how
       -r     compare subdirectories too, recursively
       -N     a file that is in only one directory (or a file named
              on the command line that does not exist) is compared
              with an empty file

       Options may be combined (-rq). Files containing NUL bytes are
       not compared line by line: diff only says "Binary files a and
       b differ".

EXAMPLES
       diff old.c new.c
       diff -u old.c new.c > fix.diff
       diff -e old new > script; (cat script; echo w) | ed - old
       diff -rq /usr/src /mnt/usr/src
       sort a | diff - b

EXIT STATUS
       0 if the files (or trees) are the same; 1 if they differ; 2
       for trouble: a file that could not be read or a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       cmp(1), ed(1), uniq(1)

BUGS
       The dates in the -u and -c headers are the files' times of
       modification, as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS in local time (the
       current time for the standard input), without fractions or
       a zone.

       An ed script cannot express a missing newline at the end.

       With -rN a subdirectory in only one tree is listed (Only
       in), not compared with an empty one.

       Both files are held in memory, with a few words for each
       line: files of tens of thousands of lines fit. The time
       taken grows with the product of their size and the number
       of differences, so two large, wholly different files are
       slow.

       There is no -s, -y (side by side), -p, -x or -I.
